# .cursorrules — OnForm Internal Tool

> SOURCE OF TRUTH. Re-read the relevant section before EVERY action.
> Companion doc: `cursor-teaching-guide.md` (long-form tutorial).
> This file is the operational rulebook; the guide is the learning narrative.
> Do not invent steps that contradict this file. If something is missing here,
> ask the user before improvising.

---

## 0. HOW I (CURSOR) MUST OPERATE ON THIS PROJECT

1. Before any task, scan this file for the relevant section (rules, current phase, env vars, troubleshooting).
2. Default mode is **teaching, not autopilot**. The user is a beginner with Next.js. When asked to build, ALWAYS:
   - Explain what each file does as I create it.
   - Use TypeScript everywhere.
   - Read existing files before editing.
3. Two-pass learning method is the workflow:
   - **Pass 1 (Build):** Cursor writes code, user reads and runs. Goal: working app.
   - **Pass 2 (Teach):** Delete files, rebuild together one at a time, quiz the user.
   - Do not skip Pass 2.
4. Always test locally (`npm run dev` on `localhost:3000`) before pushing.
5. Always commit at the end of a working phase with the exact commit message specified for that phase.
6. If a step fails: paste the error back, do not guess silently.
7. Never put secrets in code. Ever. They live in `.env.local` (dev) and Vercel env vars (prod).
8. Never expose BigQuery credentials to the browser. All BigQuery calls go through `app/api/...` server routes.
9. Authentication check happens FIRST in every API route. Role check FIRST in every admin route.
10. One commit per working feature. If something breaks: `git reset --hard HEAD~1`.

---

## 1. WHAT WE'RE BUILDING

A web app for the OnForm accountancy team that:
- **Login** — Clerk (Google, email, magic links)
- **Dashboard** — list of all clients with health metrics
- **Client detail page** — drill into one client's P&L, runway, budget
- **Admin panel** — invite team members, manage roles, view audit log
- **Exports** — download client reports as Excel / PDF
- **Connections** — one-click open into the client's Xero / Shopify / Data Studio

**Reads from BigQuery warehouse — never writes back.** Read-only by design = secure by design.

Outcome: a production-ready internal app reading from BigQuery.
Total time: 4–6 hours, split across phases.

---

## 2. ARCHITECTURE

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   USER'S BROWSER                             │
│   Next.js Frontend (React components, Tailwind UI)           │
│                          ↓ HTTPS                             │
└──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┘
                           │
┌──────────────────────────▼───────────────────────────────────┐
│         NEXT.JS API LAYER (server-side, Vercel)              │
│   • Clerk middleware verifies every request                  │
│   • Rate limiting (basic)                                    │
│   • Audit logging for sensitive actions                      │
└────────┬───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────┘
         │                               │
         ▼                               ▼
┌──────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐
│  PostgreSQL          │         │   BigQuery             │
│  (Railway)           │         │   (read-only)          │
│  • users             │         │   • clients            │
│  • profiles          │         │   • transactions       │
│  • invitations       │         │   • v_profit_loss      │
│  • audit_logs        │         │   • v_cash_runway      │
│                      │         │   • v_budget_vs_actual │
└──────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘
```

**Rule of separation:** PostgreSQL holds *app data* (who can use the app, what they've done). BigQuery holds *business data* (clients' financial numbers). They never mix.

